1. history Command

history shows commands that you previously executed in your shell.

Try:

history

You may see:

  101  pwd
  102  ls
  103  cd /tmp
  104  df -h
  105  history

The numbers are called history numbers.

=> Why is history useful?

Suppose yesterday you ran a complicated command:

ansible-playbook -i inventory playbook.yml --ask-vault-pass

But today you don't remember the exact command.

You can search your command history instead of typing it again.

=> Lab 1: Basic history

Run:

pwd
ls
df -h
free -h
uptime

Now:

history

You should see these commands.

=> Lab 2: Show last 10 commands
history 10

This means:

Show my last 10 commands.

=> Lab 3: Search history using grep

This is one of the most useful ways to deal with history.

For example:

history | grep ansible

You might get:

  250  ansible --version
  251  ansible all -m ping
  255  ansible-playbook site.yml

Search for Docker:

history | grep docker

=> Lab 4: Re-run a command using history number

Suppose:

history

shows:

300  df -h
301  free -h
302  uptime

You can execute command 300 again:

!300

This runs:

df -h

=> Lab 5: Run the previous command

First you run:

df -h

Then you type:

!!

Linux understands !! as:

“Take the command I just ran and execute it again.”

So:

!!

becomes:

df -h

=> !!

becomes:

df -h

=> Lab 6: Search previous commands interactively

Press:

Ctrl + R

Then type:

ansible

Your shell searches backward through your previous commands.

For example:

(reverse-i-search)`ansible': ansible-playbook -i inventory site.yml

If that's the command you want:

Enter

runs it.

This is probably the most useful history shortcut.

Remember:

Ctrl + R → search command history

=> Lab 7: Clear history

To clear the current shell's history:

history -c

Then:

history

You may see little or no previous history for that shell.

Note:- Important

Linux history isn't just one simple file. Bash keeps history in memory and normally writes it to:

~/.bash_history

Check it:

cat ~/.bash_history

=> Lab 8: Delete one history entry

Suppose:

history

shows:

500  ls
501  df -h
502  free -h

You can delete entry 501:

history -d 501
